# wc - count the lines, words and bytes in files

## SYNOPSIS
    wc [-lwcL] [file | pattern | - ...]

## DESCRIPTION
Counts what is in each file: lines, words and bytes, then the name.
When several files are counted, a total comes last. With no file, or
`-`, wc counts its input.

With only one count asked for, and one file or the input, wc prints the
number alone, handy in scripts: `List | wc -l`.

A word is anything between spaces, tabs or line ends.

## OPTIONS
- **-l** the number of lines.
- **-w** the number of words.
- **-c** the number of bytes. **-m** is the same (one byte is one
  character in Latin-1).
- **-L** the length of the longest line (tabs count to the next column
  of 8).

## EXAMPLES
    wc S:Startup-Sequence
    wc -l Work:Projects/#?.c
    ls -1 RAM: | wc -l
    grep -i error RAM:debug.log | wc -l
    wc -L Work:Docs/readme.txt

## RETURN CODES
0 fine, 5 a file could not be read or a pattern matched nothing,
10 bad arguments, 20 stopped with Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
Patterns may be written `#?` or `\*`; upper and lower case are the same.

`wc ?` shows the usage line.

## SEE ALSO
`cat`, `grep`, `head`, `tail`, `uniq`
